University of Cologne&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Title:&amp;nbsp;Virgins frozen in time: evolution of animal biodiversity in extreme environments&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Abstract:&amp;nbsp;Animals have evolved different strategies to cope with harsh environmental conditions. These include cryptobiosis, which can, for example, be found in some tardigrades, rotifers, and nematodes. Under desiccation or freezing cryptobiotic soil organisms arrest their metabolism completely, lose almost all cellular water, and reside in that state until favourable conditions return. As an extreme example, we revived one such nematode from Siberian permafrost after 46,000 years. The species is also parthenogenetic, and this combination of traits is quite common: cryptobiosis and asexuality have arisen together repeatedly across the invertebrate tree of life.&lt;br /&gt;
Nematodes globally make up much of the diversity of soils. Around 28,000 nematode species are formally described against estimates running into the millions, and fewer than 500 genomes are public. My group works on this gap, studying nematode biodiversity across the globe, with a particular focus on extreme environments, like the Atacama and the Namib deserts or the Australian Outback.&lt;br /&gt;
In the Atacama, the driest non-polar desert on earth, we sampled nematode communities along gradients of aridity and elevation. We find that climate and reproductive mode together shape these communities, with parthenogenetic lineages dominating the harshest sites. We are now polarising these findings by looking at the Namib, a younger and slightly less dry west-coast desert.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Some of the asexual lineages are also triploid. Polyploidy is widespread across the tree of life, but not very common in Metazoa. Consequences for genome architecture, adaptation and organismal resilience remain poorly understood in polyploid animals. Triploid parthenogenetic species present a particularly fascinating system, as there are three divergent homeologous copies of every gene, no meiotic recombination to homogenise them, and instant fixation of genomic states. We study this from an evolutionary perspective using Panagrolaimus nematodes, a genus in which triploidy arose through hybridisation independently twice and is invariably associated with parthenogenesis, while the sexual species in the genus are diploid. We generated phased chromosome-level assemblies resolving diploid genomes of sexuals and all three homeologs in asexuals. These reveal extensive intra- and interchromosomal rearrangements between haplotypes, structural divergence absent in diploid sexuals, accumulating freely in the absence of recombination. Whether the cryptobiotic lifestyle amplifies this plasticity is an open question we are currently addressing: repeated cycles of desiccation and freezing might induce DNA breakage, repaired by a permissive machinery that joins previously non-contiguous fragments.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
Together, parthenogenesis and cryptobiosis create a genomic environment of exceptional plasticity, with implications for how these nematodes persist and adapt in some of the most extreme environments on earth.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Host: Oliver Hobert&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;Have you ever lost access to project research or couldn&amp;#39;t find something in LabArchives saved by someone in your lab? Join us for a practical tour of &lt;a href="https://labnotebooks.columbia.edu/" target="_blank"&gt;LabArchives&lt;/a&gt; and learn how to manage your workspaces in sustainable, searchable, and replicable ways based on IT and Library expertise and experience.&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We&amp;rsquo;ll walk through practical methods for organizing your electronic notebook hierarchy using user roles to support collaboration and research integrity, which will make your data easier to find and maintain. We&amp;rsquo;ll also discuss how to optimize your notebook naming conventions for findability, and how activity tracking features can&amp;nbsp;streamline collaboration&amp;nbsp;(and how versioning protects your data integrity).&amp;nbsp;By the end of the workshop you&amp;rsquo;ll know how to maintain your research in sustainable and accessible ways for yourself and your (current and future) lab staff.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;***********************&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;LabArchives is available at no cost to all CU community members and supports collaboration between CUIMC and University users along with allowing for external collaboration. The platform is also certified to hold sensitive identifiable data, including PII, RHI, and PHI and is IRB-recognized as a research collaboration platform&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Columbia&amp;#39;s Electronic Notebook Service is funded by CUIT and the Libraries, in partnership with the Office of the Executive Vice President for Research.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Arabic medical treatises of the tenth century explained both endemic and epidemic diseases in environmental terms. Physicians such as Abū Bakr al-Rāzī, al-Majūsī, Muḥammad al-Tamīmī, and al-Masīḥī described various kinds of corruption of air and water, the risks these posed to different populations, and the means by which physicians could protect patients through changes in regimen, household hygiene, and, more rarely, interventions in the surrounding environment. This talk explores how these medical ideas related to the environmental practices of ʿAbbasid cities. Bringing medical treatises into dialogue with chronicles, market manuals, and travel literature, it examines the relationship between theories of disease, physicians&amp;rsquo; recommendations, and historical approaches to urban hygiene and health, asking to what extent learned medicine intersected with environmental practice.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Event Speaker&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://scienceandsociety.columbia.edu/directory/elaine-van-dalen"&gt;Elaine Van Dalen&lt;/a&gt;, Assistant Professor of Middle Eastern, South Asian and African Studies at Columbia University&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Event Information&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Free and open to the public; &lt;a href="https://www.eventbrite.com/e/elaine-van-dalen-common-airs-individual-remedies-registration-1995120098923" target="_blank"&gt;registration&lt;/a&gt; required. Contact &lt;a href="mailto:scienceandsociety@columbia.edu"&gt;scienceandsociety@columbia.edu&lt;/a&gt; with any questions.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hosted by the &lt;a href="https://scienceandsociety.columbia.edu/" target="_blank"&gt;Center for Science and Society&lt;/a&gt;. This event is part of the Faculty Spotlight Event Series. This series highlights the work of Advisory Committee members.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Please join the Harriman Institute for a lecture by&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Alice Mee&lt;/strong&gt;. Moderated by&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/elise-giuliano/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Elise Giuliano&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;How can perpetrators of war crimes be held accountable when the means and methods of waging war are changing so rapidly? And what do the changing technologies and tactics used in Ukraine today mean for civilians on the ground?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Alice Mee will present recent research on war crimes and human rights violations in Ukraine from Truth Hounds, an analytical organization founded in Kyiv in 2014. She will discuss the organization&amp;rsquo;s approaches to investigating human rights violations in Ukraine and engaging with accountability mechanisms, and will also offer insight into Truth Hounds&amp;rsquo; recent findings. In particular, Alice will present the organization&amp;rsquo;s latest research on the use of short-range drone attacks on humanitarian personnel and first responders, the challenges this poses for civilian self-protection, and the ramifications for civilians in frontline communities.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Truth Hounds is a full-spectrum accountability organization founded in Kyiv in 2014, specializing in the documentation and investigation of war crimes, international crimes and other serious human rights violations in Ukraine and other conflict-affected areas. Since 2014, Truth Hounds have conducted over 90 in-depth investigations into war crimes, carried out over 300 field missions, and identified over 320 suspects of war crimes. The organization has won numerous awards, including the Sakharov Freedom Award (2025), the Human Rights Tulip (2024), and the Clooney Foundation&amp;rsquo;s Justice for Survivors Award (2023).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Join the Columbia Law School Human Rights Institute for a book talk with Wolfgang Kaleck, founder and General Secretary of the European Center for Constitutional and Human Rights (ECCHR), on his book The Power of Law.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For decades, Kaleck has worked to hold state and corporate actors accountable for serious human rights violations through courts in Germany and around the world. Drawing on this experience, he will discuss the role law can play in challenging injustice and powerful actors, as well as its limits as a tool for human rights and social change.&lt;strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;Wolfgang Kaleck &lt;/strong&gt;is a&lt;strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;leading international human rights lawyer and the &lt;strong&gt;author &lt;/strong&gt;of several books on law, justice, and accountability.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;When: &lt;/strong&gt;Tuesday, September 29 | 12:30-1:30pm&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where: &lt;/strong&gt;JG Annex&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speaker: &lt;/strong&gt;Wolfgang Kaleck, General Secretary, European Center for Constitutional and Human Rights (ECCHR)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Lunch will be served.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please register below:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speaker: &lt;/strong&gt;Dr.&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://alextownsend.net/" target="_blank"&gt;Alex Townsend&lt;/a&gt;, Cornell University&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Title:&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;quot;An Applied Mathematician with AI&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Abstract: &lt;/strong&gt;With a chatbot, an applied mathematician can delegate mundane tasks. With an AI agent, one can compute, discover, and verify. With a swarm of AI agents, the whole&amp;nbsp;research&amp;nbsp;landscape changes.&amp;nbsp;Through a series of mathematical results, I will describe how AI has increased the pace of my research, accelerated my understanding,&amp;nbsp;and led a neuroscientist to scoop me. Despite all the uncertainty, my ambitions for applied mathematics are now bigger than ever.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Bio:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Alex Townsend is an Associate Professor of Mathematics at Cornell University. His research is in numerical analysis and scientific computing, with interests including numerical linear algebra, approximation theory, dynamical systems, operator learning, and scientific machine learning.&amp;nbsp;He is also the coauthor, with Steven Strogatz, of the popular science book&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Big Math&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;In person attendance at this seminar is only open to Columbia University affiliates. External guests are welcome to attend remotely. Please contact &lt;a href="mailto:apam@columbia.edu?subject=Zoom%20link%20needed%20for%20AM%20Colloquium" target="_blank"&gt;apam@columbia.edu&lt;/a&gt; if you need the Zoom link for this seminar. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Longevity science is moving quickly from laboratory to clinic to market. Over an evening of panel discussions, join us in conversation with leading scientists, clinicians, elite sports practitioners, and business leaders as we put a spotlight on the science around longevity, its application to human health and performance, and the commercial landscape taking shape around it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The evening will explore the topic over three moderated panel discussions:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&amp;quot;What is True Longevity?&amp;quot; will examine what is proven, what is promising, and what remains speculation across multi-omics, biomarkers of aging, metabolic health, and the data behind them.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&amp;quot;Longevity in Health &amp;amp; Sports&amp;quot; will explore how longevity science is showing up in elite performance and everyday health, from recovery and injury prevention to career extension.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&amp;quot;The Business of Longevity&amp;#39; will culminate the evening and examine the business models, market dynamics, and unit economics emerging across diagnostics, wearables, clinics, and consumer health platforms.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The program will bring together perspectives from leading longevity and sports experts from &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/weillcornellmedicine/" target="_self"&gt;Weill Cornell Medicine&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/nyu-grossman-school-of-medicine/" target="_self"&gt;NYU Grossman School of Medicine&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/realmadrid/" target="_self"&gt;Real Madrid C.F.&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/olympic-channel-home-of-team-usa/" target="_self"&gt;Olympic Channel - Home of Team USA&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/guardanthealth/" target="_self"&gt;Guardant Health&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/silver-lake/" target="_self"&gt;Silver Lake&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/dexcom/" target="_self"&gt;Dexcom&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/1arq/" target="_self"&gt;ARQ&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/united-states-military-academy-at-west-point/" target="_self"&gt;United States Military Academy at West Point&lt;/a&gt;, V Brown &amp;amp; Company, &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/stevens-&amp;amp;-lee/" target="_self"&gt;Stevens &amp;amp; Lee&lt;/a&gt;, and more.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
We invite members of the Columbia community, healthcare and life sciences professionals, researchers, entrepreneurs, investors, athletes, and others interested in the future of longevity to join us for this interdisciplinary conversation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From the streets of Chile and Iran to museums grappling with restitution across Europe and the United States:&amp;nbsp;over the past decade, protest and activism have reshaped the visual arts. What does it mean for artists to respond to social movements rather than simply reflect them? How are museums confronting the colonial histories embedded in their collections? And what have we learned, a decade on, about art&amp;#39;s role in politics, memory, and resistance?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A globally oriented and locally rooted anthology, &lt;em&gt;Politics of Visual Arts in a Changing World,&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;edited by Vishakha Desai, brings together artists, scholars, activists, and museum professionals to take up these questions, tracing how artists have acted as catalysts for change and how institutions are reckoning with their pasts across protest art, museum ethics, restitution, and institutional transformation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To mark the book&amp;#39;s publication, The Committee on Global Thought is convening its contributors for a program across two conversations:&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Art, Artists, and Social Change&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;features Angela N. Carroll, Terri Gordon-Zolov, Erin L. Thompson, and&amp;nbsp;Betti-Sue Hertz;&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Museums: Restitution and its Implications&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;brings together Jessica de Alba-Ulloa and Louisa Onuoha, with video contributions from Yudhishthir&amp;nbsp;Raj Isar and&amp;nbsp;Lotte Arndt; the program closes by audience Q&amp;amp;A.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please RSVP, as seating is limited.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Join us for an in-person screening of the film&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;My One and Only&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. IIJS Film Programmer Stuart Weinstock will lead a discussion following the screening.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tuesday, September 29, at 6:00 p.m. | 617 Kent Hall&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
This Gemara-inspired mystery orbits an ensemble of characters around a young, religious mother who is convinced her husband has been replaced by an imposter. Through three intertwined story threads, &lt;em&gt;My One and Only&lt;/em&gt; examines how people change in relationships, the difference between falling in love and staying in love, and how relationships can endure.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;(104 minutes; Hebrew with English subtitles)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PKIPKdg47U8" target="_blank"&gt;Watch the trailer here&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
