&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Stress&amp;nbsp;management,&amp;nbsp;sleep,&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;nutrition&amp;nbsp;are&amp;nbsp;key&amp;nbsp;elements&amp;nbsp;of&amp;nbsp;a&amp;nbsp;student&amp;rsquo;s&amp;nbsp;well-being. Participants will learn about why these behaviors are important, strategies for incorporating them into their lives, and resources available on campus that can provide support.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Session&amp;nbsp;open&amp;nbsp;to&amp;nbsp;all&amp;nbsp;Columbia&amp;nbsp;University&amp;nbsp;students,&amp;nbsp;regardless&amp;nbsp;of&amp;nbsp;school&amp;nbsp;affiliation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Note:&amp;nbsp;This&amp;nbsp;session&amp;nbsp;will&amp;nbsp;not&amp;nbsp;be&amp;nbsp;recorded,&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;slides&amp;nbsp;are&amp;nbsp;not&amp;nbsp;shared.&amp;nbsp;If you&amp;#39;re no longer able to attend, please remove your registration.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Columbia Health makes every effort to accommodate individuals with disabilities. If you&amp;nbsp;require&amp;nbsp;accommodations&amp;nbsp;to attend this event, contact Disability Services at 212-854-2388&amp;nbsp;or&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="mailto:disability@columbia.edu" target="_blank"&gt;disability@columbia.edu&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;at least 5 days in advance of the event.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Join us for&amp;nbsp;an engaging panel discussion featuring Columbia University faculty who have recently reached significant career milestones. Panelists will reflect on their own career journeys, share lessons learned, discuss common challenges, and offer practical advice for thriving during the mid-career years. Building a successful academic career does not end with promotion: it evolves. Many faculty reach important milestones such as promotion to Associate Professor, achieving tenure, or stepping into new leadership roles only to discover that the next stage of their career brings a new set of opportunities, challenges, and decisions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Whether you are an early-career faculty member looking ahead or a mid-career faculty member considering your next steps, this session will provide candid insights, practical strategies, and an opportunity to engage with colleagues who have recently navigated these important transitions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration is required, link below.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The CUIMC Office of Faculty Professional Development and Engagement draws upon the expertise of faculty experts, offices, and departments across the University to offer comprehensive faculty professional development, mentoring opportunities, and beneficial tools to support the advancement of all faculty at CUIMC.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;TRANSFORM (TRaining And Nurturing Scholars FOr Research that is Multidisciplinary) is the Irving Institute&amp;rsquo;s educational resource, administering the interdisciplinary master&amp;rsquo;s, predoctoral, postdoctoral, and career development programs. TRANSFORM Evolution is a community of current and alumni KL2 and Irving Scholars who support one another throughout the journey of academic medicine. The program hosts educational and social events throughout the year and fosters a network of scholars dedicated to both personal and professional growth.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Gen Z is now old enough to vote, to organize, and to hold office even as it is largely absent from the institutions that make political decisions. The median age in Congress is over 60 and on the federal bench it is 68. This panel takes up the practical questions behind those figures: what makes a political organization feel open or closed to someone in their early twenties, what stands between a first-time Gen Z candidate and a place on the ballot, and how the structure of American institutions shapes both answers.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
Join us on September 30th at Columbia University for a conversation with&lt;a href="https://law.yale.edu/samuel-moyn" target="_blank"&gt; Samuel&amp;nbsp;Moyn,&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;Chancellor Kent Professor of Law and History at Yale University and author of &lt;a href="https://us.macmillan.com/books/9780374607647/gerontocracyinamerica/" target="_blank"&gt;Gerontocracy in America&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href="https://americanstudies.yale.edu/people/daniel-hosang" target="_blank"&gt;Daniel HoSang&lt;/a&gt;, Professor of American Studies and Political Science at Yale and author of &amp;quot;&lt;a href="https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2025/07/24/opinion/minority-voters-trump-right.html" target="_blank"&gt;Inside the Rise of the Multiracial Right&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;quot; They will examine the age division in American politics, barriers to youth representation, and opportunities to widen participation. &lt;a href="https://www.law.columbia.edu/faculty/suzanne-goldberg" target="_blank"&gt;Suzanne Goldberg,&lt;/a&gt; Herbert and Doris Wechsler Clinical Professor of Law at Columbia Law School,&amp;nbsp;will moderate.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
This public panel is hosted by &lt;a href="https://worldprojects.columbia.edu/" target="_blank"&gt;Columbia World Projects&lt;/a&gt;. The discussion will be followed by an audience Q&amp;amp;A. This event is free and open to the public with registration required.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The collapse of the World Trade Towers on September 11, 2001 initiated the most comprehensive research initiative on structures in American history, showcasing new engineering and materials science methodologies, and this was only the beginning. The &amp;quot;homeland security era&amp;quot; with its attendant Homeland Security Centers of Excellence, as well as private and non-profit research initiatives (and massive funding across the board) focused on counter-terrorism touched every STEM discipline, resulting in impressive research outputs. Hurricane Katrina fits into the same frame of reaction-based science funding, opening new opportunities and re-opening older ones, this time including Earth systems science, public health, social science, and humanities work focused on emergency management and resilience.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Until the homeland security enterprise turned inward and morphed into counter-immigration law enforcement it formed a juggernaut of focused scientific research rarely seen in the nation&amp;rsquo;s history. What did the efforts of this almost-two-decade-long scientific era accomplish, what were its social dimensions, and why was it so easy to unbuild? This talk contextualizes &amp;quot;Post-September 11 Science,&amp;quot; alongside the rise of anti-science and conspiracy politics that grew alongside, arguing for a new understanding of this era as continuing many of the dual use strategies of federal science funding in the Cold War.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Event Speaker&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://cssh.northeastern.edu/faculty/scott-knowles/" target="_blank"&gt;Scott Knowles&lt;/a&gt;, Research Professor of History at Northeastern University&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Event Information&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Free and open to the public; &lt;a href="https://www.eventbrite.com/e/scott-knowles-the-united-states-of-disaster-registration-1997497362382" target="_blank"&gt;registration&lt;/a&gt; required. Contact &lt;a href="mailto:scienceandsociety@columbia.edu" target="_blank"&gt;scienceandsociety@columbia.edu&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href="mailto:historyofscience@nyu.edu" target="_blank"&gt;historyofscience@nyu.edu&lt;/a&gt; for questions.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This event is part of the &lt;a href="https://scienceandsociety.columbia.edu/node/32"&gt;New York History of Science Lecture Series&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
