&lt;p&gt;Please join the Harriman Institute for a lecture by &lt;strong&gt;Marat Grinberg&lt;/strong&gt;. Moderated by &lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/mark-lipovetsky/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mark Lipovetsky&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Friedrich Gorenstein (1932&amp;ndash;2002) stands as one of the most original and uncompromising voices in twentieth century Russian literature. His novels blend fiction, philosophy, religion, and politics in a way that is unmistakably Russian, yet he tackles these very same issues as a Jewish writer, a Jewish thinker, and a fiercely independent Jewish voice. This talk will analyze Gorenstein&amp;rsquo;s philosophy and artistry through his masterwork, the novel &lt;em&gt;Psalm&lt;/em&gt;, written in Moscow in the 1970s and published in Andrew Bromfield&amp;rsquo;s English translation in 2026. &lt;em&gt;Psalm&lt;/em&gt; is a bold metaphysical narrative which follows Dan the antichrist and the executor of God&amp;rsquo;s will. As he travels through the Soviet landscapes, witnessing their devastation, he sets a cosmic drama into motion, punishing the wicked and rewarding the righteous. The talk will present &lt;em&gt;Psalm&lt;/em&gt; as specifically a work of Soviet Jewish imagination, on the one hand, and speculative fiction, on the other, which contributed to the rich world of Soviet and East European science fiction from Stanisław Lem to the Strugatsky brothers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speaker&lt;/strong&gt;: Dr. &lt;a href="https://www.pppl.gov/people/james-yang" target="_blank"&gt;James Yang&lt;/a&gt;, Princeton Plasma Physics Lab&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Title:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;quot;Fast-ion enhanced modeling of neoclassical tearing modes at NSTX and DIII-D&amp;quot;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Abstract:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;A new framework for the solution of modified Rutherford equation including fast ions is successfully applied to interpret the tearing mode stability of two discharges NSTX #134020 and DIII-D #135861. The simulated island width growth rates are in better agreement with the measured island growth rate when the fast ions are included. While constants are multiplied to the polarization current contribution terms for NSTX #134020, no constants are necessary to match the simulated and measured island width growth rates for DIII-D #135861. The estimated island frequencies appear to provide an explanation of the different constants used in the two discharges. The gradient scale lengths suggest that the fast ion contribution can become significant in plasmas with flat thermal ion density profile and steep fast ion density profile. This work is now published: Yang et al., Plasma Phys. Control. Fusion 68 085047 (2026)&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Bio:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;James is a staff research physicist at Princeton Plasma Physics Laboratory. He joined the laboratory in 2019 as a postdoctoral researcher. His research focus includes the tearing mode stability and plasma initiation in tokamaks. He serves as the Acting Head of Physics Operations for NSTX-U, the Chair of the Program Committee for the MHD Workshop, and a Joint Experiment Lead for the ITPA IOS. He served as the Deputy Coordinating Physics Operator for DIII-D from 2023 to 2024. He received his Ph.D. in Nuclear Engineering from Seoul National University in 2019.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
