&lt;p&gt;Hybrid Event&amp;nbsp;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;a href="https://usali.org/events/aiib-ten-years-on"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;AIIB: Ten Years On&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Monday, October 5, 2026 | 5:30 &amp;ndash; 6:30 PM (ET)&amp;nbsp;&lt;br /&gt;
Furman Hall Room 212 and Zoom (&lt;a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/TcbbAPonbUJbcqc69"&gt;245 Sullivan St, New York, NY 10012&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Featuring: &lt;strong&gt;Natalie Lichtenstein&lt;/strong&gt;, Inaugural General Counsel, Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank (AIIB) (Retired)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Moderator: &lt;strong&gt;Jos&amp;eacute; Enrique Alvarez&lt;/strong&gt;, Herbert and Rose Rubin Professor of International Law, New York University School of Law; Faculty Director, U.S.-Asia Law Institute, New York University School of Law&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Co-sponsored by the &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://usali.org/"&gt;&lt;em&gt;U.S.-Asia Law Institute&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; (USALI) of New York University School of Law&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;em&gt;About the event:&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As the Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank (AIIB) launched in 2016, many wondered how this new China-led multilateral development bank (MDB) would fare.&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Natalie Lichtenstein&lt;/strong&gt;, AIIB&amp;rsquo;s founding lawyer takes a look at AIIB in context, surveying 100 years of MDB legal genealogy.&amp;nbsp; What does AIIB&amp;rsquo;s first decade tell us about the combination of heritage and innovation in AIIB&amp;rsquo;s 21st-century charter?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;About the speaker:&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Natalie Lichtenstein&lt;/strong&gt; was Inaugural General Counsel at the Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank (AIIB) and the principal drafter for the AIIB Charter. A former Assistant General Counsel at the World Bank, her 30-year legal career there spanned lending operations&amp;nbsp;in Asia and senior positions in institutional governance. At the US Treasury in the 1970s, she worked on development banks and the normalization of US-China relations. She taught Chinese Law and Society at the Johns Hopkins School of Advanced International Studies (SAIS) for many years and remains a Fellow at the China Research Center. She received an AB summa cum laude in East Asian Studies from Harvard University and a JD from Harvard Law School.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration REQUIRED by 4pm on October 2, 2026 in order to attend this event.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please join the East Central European Center and the Harriman Institute for a book presentation by&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;George Gasyna&lt;/strong&gt;. Moderated by&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/christopher-caes/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Christopher Caes&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;A Time for the Province &lt;/em&gt;proposes innovative readings of historical and contemporary Polish borderlands as a complex dialogic zone, offering new ways of connecting the Polish past(s) with the country&amp;rsquo;s present and its future. Across seven chapters, as the study dives into narratives by some of Poland&amp;rsquo;s most iconic modern authors, including the 2018 Nobel Laureate Olga Tokarczuk, it engages with fields of memory and postmemory studies, postsocialist studies, Holocaust history, Jewish-Polish relations, and ethnic and migration studies. Drawing on writings of such contemporary Polish critics as Przemysław Czapliński and Krzysztof Czyżewski, postcolonial theorists including Homi Bhabha and Edward Said, and philosophers of the human environment such as Gaston Bachelard, &lt;em&gt;A Time for the Province&lt;/em&gt; revises and complicates traditional conceptions of what the province and the borderland represent and perform within Polish literary and cultural practice, reframing this space as both a contact zone and a palimpsest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A strong El Ni&amp;ntilde;o event is anticipated in the coming months, creating a unique opportunity to examine implications for climate risk, city preparedness, and community resilience.&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/ninonina.html" target="_blank"&gt;El Ni&amp;ntilde;o&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;can affect weather, precipitation, temperature, storms, drought, air quality, food systems, supply chains, energy use, and health outcomes. It is also likely to cause a marked rise in global average surface temperatures, intensifying existing climate-related stressors.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The webinar focuses on what global cities are doing to prepare, what systems are ready, where gaps remain, and what can be done to overcome them.&amp;nbsp;Practitioners, researchers, community partners, policymakers, and students working across health, emergency management, climate, energy, and infrastructure are invited to learn about:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The anticipated El Ni&amp;ntilde;o event and its potential sector-specific implications.&lt;/li&gt;
