&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Bamako&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Abderrahmane Sissako, 2007, 115 min.&lt;br /&gt;
In French and Bambara with English subtitles&lt;br /&gt;
Screening followed by a discussion with Souleymane Bachir Diagne and Gregory Mann&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Set in the Malian capital, &lt;em&gt;Bamako &lt;/em&gt;employs a fictional trial to stage a public prosecution of the World Bank and International Monetary Fund, and ultimately Western capitalism itself, facing charges they have forcibly kept a decolonized Africa impoverished and oppressed. Meanwhile, everyday life unfolds around the proceedings. Through this imagined courtroom, the film interrogates the legacies of colonialism, global inequality, and economic dependency, transforming the trial into a forum for historical reckoning and debate.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Watch the trailer &lt;a href="https://youtu.be/hgRIcBIJJZI?si=feOnkovhA08x8ND_"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Souleymane Bachir Diagne&lt;/strong&gt; is Professor Emeritus of French and of Philosophy at Columbia University. His fields of research include the history of logic and philosophy, Islamic philosophy and Sufism, African philosophy and literature, and twentieth-century French philosophy. He is the author of numerous books, including&lt;em&gt; African Art as Philosophy: Senghor, Bergson, and the Idea of Negritude&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;The Ink of the Scholars: Reflections on Philosophy in Africa&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;Open to Reason: Muslim Philosophers in Conversation with Western Tradition&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;Le fagot de ma m&amp;eacute;moire&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;From Language to Language: The Hospitality of Translation&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;Universaliser&lt;/em&gt;, and &lt;em&gt;Ubuntu&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Gregory Mann&lt;/strong&gt; is a Professor in the History Department at Columbia University.&amp;nbsp; A historian of West and francophone Africa, his work has focused largely on Mali and the Sahel in the twentieth century, including both the colonial and the postcolonial periods. His most recent book, &lt;em&gt;Africa and France: a History&lt;/em&gt;, is currently in press with Cambridge.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This film is featured in the Maison Fran&amp;ccedil;aise film series&lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://maisonfrancaise.columbia.edu/events/history-trial-0"&gt;&lt;em&gt; History on Trial&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt;. The series is curated by Shanny Peer, with contributions by Emmanuel Kattan and Jane Gaines. Additional support for the series is provided by the School of the Arts Film &amp;amp; Media Division, Alliance Program, Society of Fellows and Heyman Center for the Humanities, and Dean of Humanities.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Generative AI has notably reshaped how university educators must think about teaching in a new landscape where generative AI tools are easily accessible. This six-week seminar for graduate students gathers a community of peers with a real range of views and attitudes about this new technology.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Together, we&amp;rsquo;ll engage in a deep dive into the research literature on how students learn as a yardstick to assess the emerging research on AI&amp;rsquo;s impact on student learning. Through dialogue and experimentation participants will refine an evidence-informed and nuanced stance on the place of generative AI in higher education. Such a stance is imperative for communicating to students the &amp;ldquo;why?&amp;rdquo; behind AI-use policies and increasingly important on the academic job market where faculty are searching for colleagues ready to teach in an AI-infused context. We&amp;#39;ll unpack how these tools work and develop informed strategies for incorporating or limiting generative AI within educational spaces.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Meetings will be on the following Wednesdays from 9:10am to 11:00am in Butler Library:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;10/7&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;10/14&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;10/21&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;10/28&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;11/11 (Please note that there is no meeting on 11/4)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;11/18&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Applications will be accepted from September 18th to September 28th.&lt;/strong&gt; Access to the application and learn more on the &lt;a href="https://ctl.columbia.edu/graduate-instructors/programs-for-graduate-students/seminars-institutes-for-graduate-students-2/critical-ai-literacy-seminar/" target="_blank"&gt;Human Learning, Artificial Intelligence Seminar website&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Participants who attend and participate across all six weeks and complete related activities receive a letter from CTL certifying successful completion that can be referenced on CVs and other descriptions of pedagogical preparation.&lt;/p&gt;
