&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Passion of Joan of Arc (La Passion de Jeanne d&amp;rsquo;Arc)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Carl Theodor Dreyer, 1928, 110 min.&lt;br /&gt;
Silent film screening with live music by Steve Sterner, following by discussion with Christia Mercer and Nico Baumbach&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The Passion of Joan of Arc&lt;/em&gt; draws upon the surviving transcripts of Joan&amp;#39;s 1431 trial, one of the most remarkable documentary records of the Middle Ages. Through Joan&amp;#39;s testimony and the probing questions of her inquisitors, the film reveals not only an extraordinary individual but also the religious beliefs, legal reasoning, and gender assumptions of late medieval Europe. This 1928 silent film directed by Carl Theodor Dreyer is widely regarded as a landmark of cinema, and Ren&amp;eacute;e Jeanne Falconetti&amp;rsquo;s performance as Joan is considered one of the most astonishing performances committed to film.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Watch the trailer &lt;a href="https://youtu.be/1mbsGj2WS1s?si=vtkkcPmFyBxcczyf"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Steve Sterner&lt;/strong&gt;, the &amp;ldquo;Piano Man of a Silent Screen&amp;rdquo; according to the&lt;a href="https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/07/nyregion/piano-man-of-a-silent-screen.html__;!!BDUfV1Et5lrpZQ!TC9rIfTdeVKosxDPlGrr-f8ld9q_F_1SF6bD9qT15CnJh705G8Wa0iEtPurqRPMYTnh9_7R-b2UIVipJg-_fpf02tg$" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;nbsp;New York Times&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, has long been charming audiences as a silent-film accompanist at film festivals and screenings at MOMA, Film Forum, BAM and elsewhere in New York City.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Nico Baumbach&lt;/strong&gt; holds a Ph.D. in Literature from Duke University. His research and teaching focus on critical theory, film theory, documentary, and the intersection of aesthetic and political philosophy. His first book, &lt;em&gt;Cinema/Politics/Philosophy&lt;/em&gt;, was released by Columbia University Press in 2019. He is currently finishing a book titled &lt;em&gt;The Anonymous Image&lt;/em&gt; and working on another project on conspiracy theory and critical theory.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Christia Mercer&lt;/strong&gt; is the Gustave M. Berne Professor of Philosophy at Columbia University and Director of the Center for New Narratives in Philosophy. Her research focuses on early modern philosophy, the history of Platonism, gender, and late medieval meditative traditions. Her work has helped broaden and rethink the history of philosophy, with particular attention to women philosophers and traditions that have been overlooked in conventional accounts of the discipline. She is the author of &lt;em&gt;Leibniz&amp;rsquo;s Metaphysics: Its Origins and Development&lt;/em&gt; and editor of the &lt;em&gt;Oxford Philosophical Concepts series&lt;/em&gt;, as well as co-editor, with Melvin Rogers, of &lt;em&gt;Oxford New Histories of Philosophy&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This film is featured in the Maison Fran&amp;ccedil;aise film series &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://maisonfrancaise.columbia.edu/events/history-trial-0"&gt;&lt;em&gt;History on Trial.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; The series is curated by Shanny Peer, with contributions by Emmanuel Kattan and Jane Gaines. Additional support for the series is provided by the School of the Arts Film &amp;amp; Media Division, Alliance Program, Society of Fellows and Heyman Center for the Humanities, and Dean of Humanities.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Negotiating without Authority: Leading through Influence in High-Stakes Transformation&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The most important negotiations in organizations rarely happen across a conference table; they happen every day as leaders align stakeholders with competing priorities, incentives, and perspectives. Drawing on nearly two decades of experience leading global transformations, mergers and acquisitions, and private equity initiatives, Ashish Malviya will share practical frameworks for influencing decisions when formal authority is limited. Through real-world case studies and lessons learned from complex business environments, participants will explore how trust, stakeholder alignment, and strategic communication can be used to navigate conflict, build consensus, and lead meaningful change. Attendees will leave with actionable approaches they can immediately apply in their own leadership and negotiation challenges.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speakers:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rodney Erb, NECR Lecturer&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ashish Malviya, Mergers &amp;amp; Acquisitions Tech Leader&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Ashish Malviya is a technology executive, educator, and advisor with nearly 20 years of experience leading enterprise transformation, mergers and acquisitions, and digital strategy for Fortune 100 companies and private equity portfolio organizations. He currently serves as managing director at Aberdeen Advisors, where he advises executive teams and investors on technology strategy, operational transformation, and value creation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In addition to his industry leadership, Ashish teaches technology strategy and data-driven decision-making courses at the University of Washington Information School. His work sits at the intersection of leadership, negotiation, and organizational change, with a focus on helping leaders build alignment across diverse stakeholders, influence without authority, and deliver complex business outcomes. Ashish is also a member of the AP Development Committee for the College Board, contributing to the development of the next generation of practical education.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;CTLgrads Learning Community - &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Scaffolding Historical Consciousness in the Classroom &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;(Session 1) - for graduate students&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Developing students&amp;rsquo; historical consciousness is a pedagogically valuable goal and applicable across all disciplines. This Learning Community explores the affordances of historical thinking as a pedagogical resource, as well as the challenges of incorporating historical thinking into teaching and learning. Coming from Architectural History and Music Theory, Kian and Jon bring firsthand experience thinking historically in their own fields, and are motivated to explore it further. Attendees will consider thinking historically as a pedagogical resource, discuss different strategies for incorporating historical thinking in course design, and workshop techniques towards utilizing history in the classroom as a learning tool..&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;This is the first session of two-part Learning Community is in-person&lt;/strong&gt; and will be co-facilitated by Jon Crompton and Kian Hosseinnia, Senior Lead Teaching Fellows at the Center for Teaching and Learning Community. Participants should register for and attend &lt;strong&gt;both&lt;/strong&gt; sessions of this LC.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;In Session 1&lt;/strong&gt;, attendees will consider what history looks like, both in their discipline/field, and more generally. For example: &amp;ldquo;history&amp;rdquo; may take the form of courses specifically designed around the history of a discipline (e.g., a proseminar in art history), may treat a specific &amp;ldquo;history&amp;rdquo; as a course topic (eg., architecture in Barcelona ca. 1800&amp;ndash;1850), or establish &amp;ldquo;history&amp;rdquo; as a contested domain (historiographical discussions concerning which histories are prioritized, legitimate, etc.). Historical thinking along these lines can also occur in smaller doses within courses that are not explicitly historically focussed. Further, attendees will be invited to reflect on their own motivations regarding teaching historically and to consider why such thinking is important. What are the pedagogical benefits of thinking historically? If historical thinking has not been part of a specific discipline, why not? Finally, we will discuss the challenges/barriers to cultivating historical consciousness in our students. For example, does introducing historical ideas clash with students&amp;rsquo; incentives for rapid professionalization?&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Session 1 will convene on Friday, 10/09, 1:10-2:25pm in room 212 Butler Library.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Session 2 will convene on Friday, 10/16, 1:10-2:25pm in room 212 Butler Library; registration is available &lt;a href="https://events.columbia.edu/cal/event/eventView.do?b=de&amp;amp;calPath=%2Fpublic%2Fcals%2FMainCal&amp;amp;guid=CAL-00bbdb7c-a0a4219b-01a0-a550afa0-000024c7events@columbia.edu&amp;amp;recurrenceId=" target="_blank"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each session of this LC counts as a Pedagogy Workshop for the &lt;a href="https://tdp.ctl.columbia.edu/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Teaching Development Program (TDP)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. See the &lt;a href="https://tdp.ctl.columbia.edu/about/faqs/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TDP FAQs&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; for more information about obtaining credit for this and other graduate student-led events through the CTL.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration REQUIRED by 4pm on October 8, 2026 in order to attend this event.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please join the Harriman Institute for a panel discussion featuring the editors and translators of&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;A Hundred Years of Childhood: An Anthology of Russian Writing for Children, 1917-2017&lt;/em&gt;. Moderated by &lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/mark-lipovetsky/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mark Lipovetsky&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;A Hundred Years of Childhood: An Anthology of Russian Writing for Children, 1917-2017&lt;/em&gt; is the first of two volumes featuring works for children and teens in English translation, many appearing in English for the first time. The volume includes poetry and prose for young children written by thirty-six renowned authors from the twentieth and early twenty-first centuries, translated by eighteen translators. The second volume, &lt;em&gt;Coming of Age in a Complicated Century: An Anthology of Russian Writing for Teens, 1917-2017&lt;/em&gt;, consisting of works for adolescents, will be published this year. The collection&amp;rsquo;s editors, Olga Bukhina, Kelly Herold, and Andrea Lanoux, along with four of the collection&amp;rsquo;s translators &amp;mdash; Jane Bugaeva, Sibelan Forrester, Ainsley Morse, and Matvei Yankelevich &amp;mdash; will discuss textual selection for both volumes, as well as the diverse translation strategies reflected in the books. Additionally, they will read excerpts from the texts in English and Russian, including Soviet children&amp;rsquo;s classics and works by contemporary authors who are still publishing today.&lt;/p&gt;
